 now well i think what keystone  was  was and i guess is targeted at would be  new projects new production  and so right now given the current  pricing environment  we don't have any new projects currently  under construction  and let's not forget that the trans  mountain pipeline which canada bought  is being expanded trans mountain is more  than enough extra capacity  for uh the oil we're going to want to  export now trudeau might want to  convince biden to commit to developing a  integrated north american plan that  would allow both countries  to exploit oil and gas resources and  commit to rapidly reducing greenhouse  gas emissions  that's a tough order and there's no  guarantee that if they did such a plan  that a keystone or a successor would  even be part of it  donna all right david aiken thanks  coming up the new philosophy of the joe  biden administration  president biden said in his inaugural  address yesterday he intends to rebuild  alliances with the rest of the world and  restore america's reputation as a beacon  of hope  today his administration thanked the  world health organization for leading  the global response to the pandemic  that came just hours after the u.s  rejoined the paris climate agreement  as mike armstrong explains biden is busy  undoing  what donald trump did  one thing the new american president has  going for him is  good will joe biden's return to the  international stage  a welcome change for many international  leaders  after four long years europe has a  friend in the white house  the trump administration pushed the  slogan america first but the effect was  basically  america alone some have called it the  withdrawal doctrine  under trump the u.s pulled out of  international trade agreements treaties  and organizations  often with strong rhetoric as it walked  out the door  the paris accord was not designed to  save the environment that was designed  to kill  the american economy biden has wasted no  time  re-engaging the us in some of what trump  pulled out of he rejoined the paris  accord on day one  leading to an apology on day two this is  climate envoy john kerry speaking to a  g20 forum he said  with pain and embarrassment for the last  four years  the leader of our country chose to  pull out of the agreement and frankly  engage in  reckless behavior the biden  administration made it official at the  world health organization thursday as  well dr anthony fauci now working for  the biden white house saying it won't  pull out of the who  as president trump had announced this is  a good day for who  and a good day for global health a lot  of what  trump did is reversible analyst michael  o'hanlon says  biden can undo most of the damage done  to u.s relationships including canada  the new president is a known commodity  and well liked by traditional allies  but will the us be trusted like before  if countries think agreements can just  be ripped up by each new administration  that part is probably going to take a  little time to repair  and especially when you have to worry  that trump or someone like him may come  back  now parts of biden's to-do list are  tougher than others like the iran  nuclear deal some things will be more  complicated than just saying  we're back in mike armstrong global news  washington  there were a few notable fashion moments  at yesterday's inauguration but it isn't  lady gaga getting all the attention  today  it's a 79 year old senator from vermont  bernie sanders seems to live by the rule  that you only need  one good coat his old standby along with  a pair of fluffy knitted mittens made of  wool from old sweaters  sparked a storm of social media memes  he's been pasted into all kinds of  scenes from star trek  to fine art even lounging around a bc  brew pub  there's a website where you can put them  anywhere in the world  when asked about all the attention  sanders said in vermont we're not so  concerned with good  fashion we want to keep warm  words to live by global national is back  in a minute  boeing's 737 max returned to canadian  skies today  the planes were grounded worldwide  almost two years ago  this westjet flight took off from  calgary this morning bound for vancouver  air canada will resume flights on its  737 max jets on february  1st transport canada cleared the boeing  plains after an extensive safety review  announcing mandatory upgrades of  software and advanced pilot training to  fix the issues that caused  the two fatal crashes in indonesia and  ethiopia  346 people were killed including 18  canadians  the big question though is will enough  people  get back on board redmond shannon  reports  boeing desperately needs its best seller  to thrive again  the plane maker airlines and agencies  like transport canada have been working  since 2019 to get the 737 max  back in the skies they have modified its  software and pilot training  key factors in the two fatal crashes  canada has even taken the unusual  measure of adding extra training  requirements and cockpit procedures for  the three canadian airlines that fly the  max  i'd get in this aircraft and fly any one  of them for the rest of my life  without any qualms at all about the  safety  i think you're going to have trouble  getting people on board the  737 max 7 or max 8  but not because there's anything wrong  with it it just it has a bad name  in the early 1960s the lockheed electro  was the victim of a bad name  following three crashes the public lost  confidence and airlines gave up on the  plane  a westjet passenger survey suggests  two-thirds of canadians want to avoid  flying in the max  at westjet we put safety above all  canadian airlines will tell passengers  if they are booking on a max  air canada and westjet are initially  offering free flight changes if  passengers feel uncomfortable  flying on the plane you have to get the  aircraft into the air  and flying um you know and and  and flying safely you know and that in  itself  is is ultimately what's going to to  build confidence here  boeing knew of some of the max's issues  before the crashes and didn't tell  airlines it has since paid out three  billion dollars in fines and  compensation  rebuilding its max brand may eat even  further into its sales revenue  if you have to take a short-term hit on  the prices paid  then that will be regained in the future  as people begin to fly on the more often  the max is already flying in the u.s and  latin america  it's cleared for takeoff in europe next  week redmond shannon global news  london  post infection immunity next researchers  find hope in the antibodies of covid  survivors  over the course of the pandemic more  than six hundred thousand canadians have  become infected with covid19  and recovered it is assumed they are  immune from getting infected again  but it is not clear for how long  researchers in toronto have been trying  to answer that critical question  and as jeff semple reports their  findings so far are providing  much needed good news over the past year  this has become hart jackson's routine  providing regular blood samples and  helping to answer a key question  in the fight against covid19 part of it  was  to know that we had the immune response  still the cancer researcher and his  family tested positive for covid19  back in march after recovering he  learned his hospital colleagues were  developing  an antibody test to determine if people  who'd previously been infected with  covid19  are now immune as soon as the the trial  started i was  signing up right away and hoping to get  involved and provide any samples that i  can  now 10 months since his infection  heart's covid19 antibodies  remain robust and researchers are  optimistic  the expectation is and of course they're  continuing their studies  that the the immunity will last for at  least a year  hopefully multiple years recent studies  suggest covid19 antibodies peak  after four weeks but remain relatively  stable for at least eight months  so it still declines a little bit but it  declines very very slowly  and even after antibodies drop they may  still provide some immunity  a study of the virus in monkeys found  that even relatively low  antibodies were sufficient for  protection the immune response is  durable  however a caveat there is that there's a  lot of variability in that immune  response from person to person  those who experience mild symptoms for  example may have  fewer antibodies and a small number have  already become  re-infected for that reason  immunologists say everyone should  receive the vaccine  even if they've had the virus now i  would suggest that those people maybe  not be at the front of the line but it  won't hurt to get the vaccine and i  think it's overall it's recommended  and vaccine immunity is also looking  promising  after four months antibodies from the  moderna vaccine were at similar levels  to those with natural immunity  providing covid19 researchers with a  much needed  shot in the arm jeff sample global news  toronto  and that is global national for this  thursday i'm donna friesen we leave you  with a bit of a different your canada  tonight  ontario's premier trying his hand at  multiple languages to deliver  the same message stay home thanks for  watching  take care of yourselves stay at  home rest day ala maison daisai jolly  low fun hi okay  ecasa  iso
 

